config VS_VOICE_MAX_TURNS
	int "VelaSight idle assistant maximum turns per conversation"
	default 20
	range 2 100
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Local cap on how many question/answer turns one conversation can
		retain.  Every accepted turn is kept verbatim for the final CHAT
		record even after its replay context has been semantically compacted.

config VS_VOICE_TURN_STORAGE_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight retained full-turn storage budget (bytes)"
	default 131072
	range 16384 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Hard aggregate heap budget for the complete UTF-8 questions and
		answers retained until CHAT persistence.  The implementation never
		clips a turn to fit this budget: an answer that would exceed it is
		rejected explicitly and all earlier accepted turns are still saved.

config VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight idle assistant conversation context budget (bytes)"
	default 65536
	range 8192 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the JSON-escaped conversation context replayed into
		the next model request.  At the configured percentage, prior turns
		are replaced in the prompt by a semantic summary.  Their complete
		text remains in the separate retained-turn budget above so the final
		CHAT history record is lossless.

config VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_COMPACT_PERCENT
	int "VelaSight idle assistant context compaction threshold (percent)"
	default 80
	range 50 95
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Percentage of CONFIG_VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES at which the
		running conversation is compacted (see above).  The product
		requirement this implements is stated in terms of the model's
		own maximum context (mimo-v2.5 advertises a 1M-token window);
		CONFIG_VS_VOICE_CONTEXT_MAX_BYTES is deliberately a much smaller,
		locally-enforced budget, because this device's own conversation
		length in one sitting never approaches a million tokens and a
		budget sized to it would make local RAM the only limit that
		exists in practice.  See vs_voice.c's chat-context module for the
		full reasoning.
config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_RING_BYTES
	int "VelaSight TTS playback staging ring (bytes)"
	default 655360
	range 16384 2097152
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Size of the ring vs_audio.c stages TTS audio in, taken from PSRAM
		with the SRAM heap only as a fallback.

		The DAC pipeline itself is only CONFIG_AUDIO_NUM_BUFFERS x
		CONFIG_AUDIO_BUFFER_NUMBYTES, which is a quarter of a second at
		16 kHz.  Writing the WebSocket's PCM straight into that would pace
		the network at the speed of the speaker.

		The default is 20 seconds of 16 kHz mono audio, which is what the
		PSRAM heap can actually spare: measured on hardware it has about
		900 KiB free at the point playback opens, so asking for the 40
		seconds that would hold the longest possible answer only produced
		a halving retry and a warning on every reply.

		A ring that fills is no longer dangerous, only slow.  Since the
		client answers the server's keep-alive pings, a blocked write
		merely applies backpressure and the reply arrives at the speed it
		is spoken.  What the size still buys is headroom against the
		opposite problem: audio arriving slower than it plays, which is
		what CONFIG_VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MS addresses.

config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MS
	int "VelaSight TTS playback pre-buffer (ms)"
	default 1000
	range 0 10000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much audio to accumulate before the DAC is allowed to start
		consuming it.

		The failure this prevents is the opposite of a full ring.  The TTS
		service produces speech in bursts and slower than real time -- one
		measured reply delivered about four seconds of audio over five and
		a half seconds of wall clock -- so a player that starts on the
		first chunk runs the ring dry between bursts.  The driver handles
		that without complaining: its transmit interrupt masks itself when
		no buffer is queued and re-arms on the next one, so an empty ring
		is silence rather than an error, and the reply comes out audibly
		chopped.

		One second is enough for the arrival rate measured here.  With
		audio arriving at a fraction r of real time, a reply of T seconds
		needs T(1-r)/r of head start to play without a gap; the measured
		r was 0.73 over a 2.2 second reply, which needs 0.8 s.

		It was 3000, and that was worse than useless: a whole reply is
		often smaller than three seconds of audio, so the level never
		reached the mark and only vs_audio_playback_drain() could release
		the gate -- which happens after the TTS stream ends, five seconds
		later than the audio was actually in hand.  A timeout now releases
		the gate as well, so a short reply no longer waits for audio that
		is never coming.

		Zero starts playback on the first chunk.

		This is the starting value and the floor. The actual watermark
		adapts at runtime between this and
		CONFIG_VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MAX_MS.

config VS_AUDIO_PLAYBACK_PREBUFFER_MAX_MS
	int "VelaSight TTS playback pre-buffer ceiling (ms)"
	default 4000
	range 0 10000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the adaptive playback watermark.

		The watermark doubles after any reply that starved and decays
		slowly after replies that did not, because the arrival rate is a
		property of the service and the link rather than of one sentence --
		measured on this hardware it swings between 0.60x and 1.93x real
		time, so no single value is right for both.  It is remembered
		across replies; relearning it per sentence would starve the first
		one every time.

		The ceiling exists because the watermark is also the delay before
		a reply starts speaking.  Beyond a few seconds it is better to
		accept a gap than to make the user wait, and a link that cannot
		sustain speech at four seconds of head start has a problem this
		cannot paper over.

config VS_VOICE_TTS_SPEAKER
	string "VelaSight TTS voice (Volcengine voice_type)"
	default "zh_female_meilinvyou_emo_v2_mars_bigtts"
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Volcengine voice_type used for spoken replies, seeded into the
		agent config store next to the ASR cluster.  The product default is
		the female voice zh_female_meilinvyou_emo_v2_mars_bigtts, which is
		what Volcengine's own reference client uses and which pairs with the
		volcano_tts cluster this firmware sends.  Leave empty to fall back
		to packages/ai_agent's compiled-in male voice,
		zh_male_beijingxiaoye_emo_v2_mars_bigtts.

		Stay within the emo_v2_mars_bigtts family: a voice from another
		family such as *_moon_bigtts belongs to a different cluster and the
		request, which sends cluster=volcano_tts, would be refused.

		Voices are entitlements, not just identifiers: an account that has
		not activated the one named here is refused, and the refusal shows
		up as "[volc_tts_ws] server error" in the log rather than as a
		configuration complaint.  Check the console for the voices your
		app_id actually has, and put one of those here.

		Known-good fallback if this voice is not activated:
		zh_male_beijingxiaoye_emo_v2_mars_bigtts

config VS_AUDIO_CAPTURE_SETTLE_BUFFERS
	int "VelaSight capture buffers discarded while the ADC settles"
	default 2
	range 0 32
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on how many driver buffers may be dropped at the start
		of a recording because the analog front end is still settling.

		This is a ceiling, not a quota.  Only buffers that are saturated
		almost sample for sample are actually dropped, and the first
		buffer that is not ends the phase, so an utterance keeps its
		opening syllables even when the user starts talking immediately.

		Kept deliberately low.  The second reason this used to exist -- a
		local VAD reading full-scale settling noise as speech -- is gone
		along with the VAD, and the remaining risk runs the other way:
		every buffer dropped here is audio the recognizer never sees, and
		the recognizer handles a short burst of noise far better than it
		handles a missing first word.  At the default 2048-byte buffer this
		bounds the loss at about 128 ms of 16 kHz mono audio.

config VS_VOICE_ASR_CLUSTER
	string "Volcengine streaming ASR cluster"
	default "volcengine_streaming_common"
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Cluster name sent in the streaming ASR request, written into
		ai_agent's config store next to the provisioned app_id and token.

		The service turns this into the entitlement it checks.
		"volcengine_streaming_common" maps to resource id
		volc.streamingasr.common.cn, and an app that has not been granted
		that resource is refused with HTTP 403 and backend_code 45000030
		("requested resource not granted") -- note that this is not an
		authentication failure, so the app_id and token can be perfectly
		valid and the request still be rejected.

		Change this only to match what the Volcengine console shows as
		enabled for the app whose credentials were provisioned.  Switching
		to the large-model streaming ASR service is not just a cluster
		rename: it uses a different endpoint and request protocol than
		AGENT_DOUBAO_ASR_WS_PATH implements.

config VS_AUDIO_MIC_GAIN
	int "VelaSight microphone analog pre-amplifier gain"
	default 6
	range 0 15
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Analog MIC gain sent to the capture driver before it configures the
		ADC (its private BK7258_AUDIOIOC_SET_CAPGAIN ioctl).

		This stage sits ahead of the ADC, so it is the only gain that
		improves the signal-to-noise ratio -- and the only one that can
		clip irrecoverably.  Tune it against the "capture level" line the
		voice worker logs after every utterance, which reports peak and RMS
		on the samples' own 0..32767 scale.

		Measured on this board, all against that full scale:

		  gain 0, empty quiet room ............ rms 28
		  gain 3, several people talking,
		          clearly audible to a person . rms 55, peak 541
		  gain 7, near-field speech ........... rms 2671, peak 32767 (clipped)

		The middle row is the case that matters and the reason the default
		is not lower: conversation a person hears without effort sat barely
		above the empty room's noise floor, and the recognizer answered
		"no valid speech" every time.  Far-field pickup simply needs much
		more gain than near-field does.

		The default trades against the third row.  It is below the setting
		that clipped on close speech while leaving room for the far-field
		case to reach a usable level.  If peak reaches 32767, lower this;
		if the recognizer still finds nothing while peak stays in the low
		thousands, raise it.  Prefer this over the digital gain below.

config VS_AUDIO_ADC_GAIN
	int "VelaSight ADC digital gain"
	default 45
	range 0 63
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Digital gain applied after the ADC, where 45 (0x2d) is 0 dB and the
		range spans roughly -45 dB to +18 dB in 1 dB steps.

		Digital gain cannot improve the signal-to-noise ratio -- it
		amplifies the noise floor with the signal -- so raise
		VS_AUDIO_MIC_GAIN first and use this only for the last few dB once
		the analog stage is as high as it can go without clipping.

config VS_AUDIO_CAPTURE_RING_BYTES
	int "VelaSight capture staging ring (bytes)"
	default 262144
	range 16384 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Size of the ring a reader thread stages captured audio in, taken
		from PSRAM with the SRAM heap only as a fallback.

		The consumer hands every chunk to the ASR service over TLS, which
		blocks.  The driver holds only CONFIG_AUDIO_NUM_BUFFERS x
		CONFIG_AUDIO_BUFFER_NUMBYTES -- about 256 ms at 16 kHz -- and its
		interrupt handler discards samples once that queue is empty,
		reporting nothing at all.  A network write that stalled longer than
		that therefore lost speech invisibly: a measured 15 s window came
		back holding 12.7 s of audio, and the recognizer saw chopped words.

		This ring is the slack between the two, so a stall costs buffer
		space instead of audio.  If the "microphone" log line at the end of
		a session reports dropped bytes, the consumer fell further behind
		than this covers.

		Raised from 131072 (4.1 s) to 262144 (8.2 s of 16 kHz mono) on
		measurement.  A 74 s social session on 2026-09-07 had the ADC
		deliver 70.5 s of audio and the ring discard 1009152 bytes of it --
		31.5 s, 44.8% -- because the drain needed about 4.1 s to encode
		each 2 s chunk, which is exactly the old ring's capacity, so it sat
		permanently on the edge of overflowing.

		Not raised further, and that is a deliberate ceiling rather than
		caution about memory.  A ring is slack for a consumer that is
		momentarily behind; it cannot help one that is behind on average,
		and the only thing extra depth buys such a consumer is a longer
		backlog.  For this session the backlog is audio the cloud has to
		align against a video timeline, so depth turns "some audio was
		lost" into "all the audio arrives progressively later than the
		frames it belongs with" -- which is worse, because it is wrong
		rather than incomplete.  524288 was tried and is what 16 s of drift
		looks like.

		8.2 s covers a stall without letting the lag become the problem.
		Whether the drain keeps up at all is decided elsewhere: by the Opus
		complexity it runs at (CONFIG_AUDIO_TEST_OGG_COMPLEXITY) and by its
		priority (VS_PRIORITY_SOCIAL_AUDIO in include/vs_types.h).

# Social-session cloud (app/velasight/vs_cloud.c).
#
# These configure the team's own /contest/v1/* server, not the MiMo or
# Volcengine services the idle assistant uses.  Only options vs_cloud.c
# actually reads are listed; the capture cadence and alert debounce options
# from the integration plan belong to vs_social.c and arrive with it.

# Where the endpoint comes from, since there are now three places it could:
#
#   1. the Web provisioning record, per field, highest priority
#   2. VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_HOST/PORT below, when the host is not empty
#   3. CONFIG_VELASIGHT_PROVISION_CLOUD_HOST/_PATH/_PORT, the factory default
#      the setup page also shows as its placeholder text
#
# The record is read once by vs_cloud_init() and again by
# vs_cloud_reload_endpoint() after a successful Web save.  It is never read on
# a request path -- a session registers an upload several times a second, and
# a file read there would put VFAT I/O between a captured frame and the socket.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_HOST
	string "VelaSight social cloud host override"
	default ""
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Hostname or IPv4 address of the social-session cloud, with no
		scheme and no port -- both of those are separate.

		Leave this empty for a normal build.  Empty no longer means
		"unconfigured": it means "use the factory default", which is
		CONFIG_VELASIGHT_PROVISION_CLOUD_HOST and is a real address.
		The Web provisioning record overrides both.

		Set it only to aim a whole build somewhere else -- a local mock
		during bring-up being the case it exists for, since a dotted quad
		here skips DNS and works before any resolver is configured.  A
		build that sets it is asking for that endpoint deliberately, so
		it wins over the factory default; it does not win over a
		provisioned record, because a device someone has actually
		provisioned should go where they said.

		If this and the factory default are both empty, the module is
		genuinely unconfigured: every operation fails with -ENODATA
		without opening a socket, and social mode refuses to start rather
		than timing out against a host that was never set.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_PORT
	int "VelaSight social cloud port override"
	default 18080
	range 1 65535
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Used only when VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_HOST above is non-empty; the two
		travel together, because a port belongs to the host it was chosen
		for.  With the host left empty this value is ignored and
		CONFIG_VELASIGHT_PROVISION_CLOUD_PORT applies.

		The default is the port used throughout the cloud interface
		document's curl examples, which is the local-mock case this
		override targets.

config VS_SOCIAL_CLOUD_TLS
	bool "Reach the social cloud over TLS"
	default n
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Send /contest/v1/* over HTTPS through packages/ai_agent's
		vela_tls.c instead of cleartext HTTP.

		Off by default because the cloud is not there yet: the interface
		document specifies cleartext for the device-facing endpoints and
		its examples are plain HTTP, and no certificate chain has been
		captured for a real host.

		Understand what turning this on currently does and does not buy.
		vela_tls.c negotiates with MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_OPTIONAL and this
		board has no CA bundle and no trusted time -- it forces the clock
		to a fixed 2026 value when it finds the year unset -- so the
		result is encryption without authentication.  That is strictly
		better than cleartext against a passive observer and no defence
		at all against an active one.  Do not describe a build with this
		enabled as having verified certificates until SNTP, a CA bundle
		and VERIFY_REQUIRED are all in place; see
		docs/plans/BK7258_OPENVELA_MIMO_NETWORK_PORTING_PLAN.md for the
		order those have to land in.

		This setting is independent of the presigned upload URL, whose
		scheme comes from the cloud's own response.

config VS_SOCIAL_DEVICE_ID
	string "VelaSight social cloud device id override"
	default ""
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Normally left empty.  The identifier is derived from the wlan0
		MAC address, which is unique and survives reboots without
		needing to be stored anywhere.

		This is the fallback for a board whose MAC cannot be read, and an
		escape hatch for cloud-side testing that wants a fixed, readable
		name.  Only letters, digits, '-', '_' and '.' are accepted;
		anything else is rejected at startup with a log line.

config VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_CONTENT_TYPE
	bool "Send Content-Type when transferring to the presigned URL"
	default n
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Whether the PUT that carries the JPEG or Ogg bytes to the
		presigned URL declares image/jpeg or audio/ogg.

		This exists as an option because object stores compute the
		presigned signature over a specific set of headers, and
		Content-Type is sometimes one of them: if it was signed, omitting
		it is rejected, and if it was not, sending it is rejected.  Both
		failures come back as HTTP 403 with an XML body.

		Default is off, because for this contest it is no longer an open
		question.  The cloud interface document's FDS upload example
		(section 7, storage) requires the header to be absent, spelling
		it as an explicit curl argument:

		  curl -i -X PUT '<presigned URL>' -H 'Content-Type:' \
		    --data-binary @/home/mi/a.jpg

		The empty -H 'Content-Type:' form is how curl suppresses a header
		it would otherwise supply, so FDS signs without it.  Sending one
		was measured (2026-08-31) as "Galaxy FDS Error: Signature Does
		Not Match" on every transfer while session open and upload
		registration both succeeded, which is the exact split this option
		was written to resolve.

		Turn it back on only for an object store known to sign the
		header; vs_cloud.c names this option in the 403 log line so the
		reverse case is equally traceable.

config VS_SOCIAL_REG_RESP_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social cloud registration response buffer (bytes)"
	default 4096
	range 1024 32768
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Buffer for the small responses: session open, upload
		registration, session close, and the object store's reply to a
		transfer.  The largest thing that lands here is a presigned URL,
		or an object store's XML error document.

		Sized separately from the result buffer below because one of
		these is allocated for every frame and every audio chunk, and
		sizing them all for a full session's timelines would waste PSRAM
		several times a second.

config VS_SOCIAL_RESP_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social cloud result response buffer (bytes)"
	default 65536
	range 8192 262144
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Buffer for getResult.  The end-of-session response is the large
		one: it carries the text minutes plus a full emotion timeline and
		a full audio timeline, one entry per sampled frame and per
		recognised sentence, so it grows with session length.

		A session whose minutes exceed this is reported as -E2BIG rather
		than truncated, because a clipped JSON record would be persisted
		and then fail to parse afterwards. If that happens, raise this.

config VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight spoken minutes size limit (bytes on disk)"
	default 8388608
	range 65536 67108864
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the spoken minutes fetched from ttsMinutes and
		written beside the record they belong to.

		This is a limit on a file, not on an allocation.  The download
		streams to storage through one 8 KB transport buffer, so raising
		this costs space on the SD-NAND and nothing in RAM.  That is the
		difference from how it used to work, and the reason it changed:

		Measured against the staging cloud on 2026-09-04, ttsMinutes is a
		RIFF/WAVE file of 16-bit signed mono PCM at 24 kHz -- 48000 bytes
		per second of speech, not the ~2 KB/s an Opus stream would cost.
		One observed reply was 599084 bytes for 12.48 seconds, and that
		was the shortest such file this cloud can produce: the session had
		detected no emotion at all, so its minutes were a single line.
		Buffering the file in memory therefore capped how long a meeting
		could be -- the 786432 byte budget it needed bought sixteen
		seconds of speech -- which is not a limit that belongs in a heap.

		8388608 is about three minutes at that rate.  Divide by 48000 to
		read the value as seconds.

		Exceeding it is not fatal: the text minutes are already on screen
		and persisted by then, the partial file is removed, and the audio
		is skipped with a log line naming this option.

config VS_SOCIAL_MINUTES_TEXT_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight social text minutes download budget (bytes)"
	default 8192
	range 512 65536
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound on the text minutes fetched from txtMinutes.

		Unlike the spoken minutes above, this one is collected in memory,
		because the caller needs the whole string at once to substitute it
		into the result JSON.  That is affordable for prose and is why the
		two limits are separate rather than shared: a few hundred bytes of
		summary must not be fetched into a multi-megabyte budget.

		The interface document shows txtMinutes carrying the summary text
		inline, but the staging cloud sends a presigned FDS URL instead
		(measured 2026-09-04, 219 characters).  Both shapes are accepted;
		this budget only applies to the URL case.

		8192 is roughly 2700 Chinese characters, well past anything the
		result page or a history card can show.

config VS_SOCIAL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social cloud connect timeout (ms)"
	default 3000
	range 500 30000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long a cleartext connect may take before it is abandoned.
		The connect is non-blocking with an explicit poll() so this is
		the real bound, not the stack's own much longer one.

		Keep it short.  A session uploads several times a second, and a
		host that has gone away must not stall the upload thread for
		longer than the queue behind it can absorb.

config VS_SOCIAL_IO_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social cloud send/receive timeout (ms)"
	default 8000
	range 1000 60000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Socket send and receive timeout for the cleartext path, once
		connected.  Larger than the connect timeout because this also
		covers the cloud thinking about a request, not just the link.

# Session orchestration (app/velasight/vs_social.c).
#
# The schedule, not the protocol: how often to sample, how much to keep when
# the network stalls, and how sure the cloud has to be before an emotion is
# worth interrupting the user for.

config VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS
	int "VelaSight social image sampling interval (ms)"
	default 500
	range 200 2000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Target gap between captured frames.  The cloud interface document
		asks for 200 ms.

		What limits this is no longer the camera.  Earlier values here
		were sized against "252 ms per delivered frame", read from
		stop_capture's validator average, and that number was wrong in
		kind: it came from clock_systime_ticks() around work that ran at
		a contended priority, so it measured wall time including
		preemption rather than cost.  CONFIG_USEC_PER_TICK is 1000, too
		coarse to see the real figure at all.  With the DWT cycle counter
		(CONFIG_ARCH_PERF_EVENTS) a whole-frame entropy walk measures
		about 35 ms, and CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_VALIDATE_MCUS brings
		that to 1-4 ms by settling alignment on a 128-macroblock prefix.
		The driver now delivers 17.39-17.87 fps at 480x480.

		The limit moved to the cloud round trip.  Each image is two of
		them -- register for a msgId and presigned URL, then PUT the
		bytes -- and one measured about 135 ms on a pooled connection.
		At 2 fps that is roughly 540 ms of round-trip time per second of
		session, which one worker can carry.  5 fps would need about
		1350 ms per second, so the document's rate needs the upload path
		to overlap requests before it means anything.

		Overshooting is no longer dangerous, only wasteful: the social
		workers run at VS_PRIORITY_SOCIAL, below the UI, so a backlog
		costs dropped frames rather than a frozen screen.  And the cloud
		stamps every emotionTimeline entry with its own timestamp, making
		no assumption about spacing, so a sparser timeline is a smaller
		timeline and not a broken one.

		Keep this equal to 1000 / VS_SOCIAL_CAPTURE_FPS.  The two knobs
		gate different stages and a mismatch wastes whole frames of
		validation on frames this application never reads -- see that
		option's help text.

		Keep this equal to 1000 / VS_SOCIAL_CAPTURE_FPS.  The two knobs
		gate different stages and a mismatch spends whole frames of
		validation on frames this application never reads -- see that
		option's help text.

		The numbers to watch are the "sampled N frames" line at the end
		of a session against the session's duration, and hw_busy against
		sampler_skipped in the driver's stop_capture line.  hw_busy above
		sampler_skipped means the pipeline is saturating rather than
		pacing, which is the signal that this value is too low for what
		the rest of the session can carry.

config VS_SOCIAL_CAPTURE_FPS
	int "VelaSight social camera delivery rate (fps)"
	default 2
	range 1 30
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		What the camera driver is asked to deliver during a session, as a
		per-stream request rather than a build setting.

		This is not the same knob as VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS above.
		That one decides which frames this application reads; this one
		decides how many the driver bothers to produce.  Without it the
		driver runs at CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_FPS -- about 16.7 fps
		measured at 480x480 -- and every frame costs a copy out of the
		drain ring plus a full 4:2:2 Huffman validation over
		non-cacheable PSRAM, in interrupt and work-queue context,
		alongside the microphone and the mailbox.  A session that reads
		two frames a second has no use for the other fifteen.

		Keep this equal to 1000 / VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS.  An
		earlier default deliberately ran this above the rate the interval
		implied, on the reasoning that the headroom keeps a fresh frame
		waiting when the capture thread comes back.  Match them instead:
		validation happens in the EOF path on everything the driver
		delivers, before this application ever looks at it, so a headroom
		frame is validated and then discarded.  At 5 delivered against
		2.94 read, about 40% of the driver's validation work was spent on
		images nobody read.

		Two.  The cost of a delivered frame is now 1-4 ms of entropy
		validation (CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_VALIDATE_MCUS settles bit
		alignment on a 128-macroblock prefix instead of the whole scan),
		so the camera is no longer what sets this.  The upload path is:
		two cloud round trips per image at about 135 ms each, which is
		roughly 540 ms per second of session at 2 fps.  Earlier defaults
		of 1 and 2 were chosen against a "252 ms per frame" figure that
		turned out to be wall time including preemption rather than cost
		-- see VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS for what replaced it.

		Overshooting is wasteful rather than dangerous.  This work runs
		at BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_PRIORITY and the cloud workers at
		VS_PRIORITY_SOCIAL, both below the UI, so a rate the session
		cannot carry shows up as jpeg_hw_busy and a growing upload queue,
		not as a screen that stops repainting.

		Set it to 0 to ask for nothing and take the driver's own rate.
		A rate the driver will not accept is not fatal -- the session
		opens at whatever the driver chose and says so in the log.

		Lowering CONFIG_BK7258_CAMERA_JPEG_FPS instead would have the
		same effect here and the wrong effect everywhere else: that
		ceiling is build-wide and /dev/video0 is shared with web_tool's
		live preview and agent_camera.

config VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_WIDTH
	int "VelaSight social frame width"
	default 480
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Must pair with VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_HEIGHT to form one of the sensor's
		three exact modes: 480x480, 640x480 or 864x480.  The driver does
		an exact match, not a nearest fit, so anything else fails at
		stream open.

		480x480 by default, the smallest of the three: this is the
		emotion path, where the face fills the frame and the encode time
		is what limits the sampling rate.

config VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_HEIGHT
	int "VelaSight social frame height"
	default 480
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT

config VS_SOCIAL_AUDIO_CHUNK_MS
	int "VelaSight social audio chunk duration (ms)"
	default 2000
	range 500 5000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much speech goes into one upload.  2000 ms is what the cloud
		interface document specifies.

		Each chunk is a complete, self-contained Ogg Opus stream, so a
		chunk that fails to upload costs only itself.  Larger chunks mean
		fewer round trips and coarser timeline resolution; smaller ones
		mean the advice for an extreme moment arrives sooner.

config VS_SOCIAL_AUDIO_BITRATE
	int "VelaSight social audio bitrate (bits/s)"
	default 24000
	range 8000 64000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Opus target bitrate for the uploaded chunks.  24 kbps is
		comfortable for 16 kHz mono speech and keeps a 2 s chunk near
		6 KB, which matters because the chunk shares the uplink with a
		frame every third of a second.

config VS_SOCIAL_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
	int "VelaSight social result poll interval (ms)"
	default 1500
	range 500 5000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How often the session asks about the messages it has in flight.

		Independent of the sampling rate on purpose.  One getResult can
		carry many msgIds, so polling does not have to keep pace with
		uploading, and a shorter interval mostly buys extra round trips
		on a link that is already carrying the uploads.

config VS_SOCIAL_FINALIZE_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social finalize timeout (ms)"
	default 90000
	range 5000 180000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long to poll for the minutes after asking the cloud to close
		the session.

		The close is asynchronous by design: the cloud answers with a
		msgId and status 40, and the minutes -- text summary, TTS URL and
		both timelines -- have to be polled for.  Generous by default
		because that work grows with the length of the conversation, and
		the alternative to waiting is showing the user a session that
		produced nothing.

		This was 30000, which measurement showed was not generous at all.
		Two runs against the staging cloud: 19.8 s on 2026-09-03 (from the
		AI端 server log) and 24.4 s on 2026-09-04 (measured end to end
		from the DELETE).  Both are the same trivial session -- a handful
		of frames, no faces found, so the summary was one sentence -- and
		they differ by nearly five seconds.  A real conversation has more
		to summarise and a voice to synthesise, and the jitter was already
		inside the old 5.6 s of headroom.

		Then 60000, chosen against that spread rather than its maximum,
		which three more measurements showed was still too tight:
		24.5 s, 44.3 s and 57.4 s on 2026-09-07.  The last one left 2.6 s
		of a 60 s budget, so the next slightly slower cloud would have
		shown the user a retry prompt for a session that was about to
		succeed.

		90000 is against the observed spread again, with the spread now
		known to reach nearly a minute.  Waiting costs a spinner; timing
		out early discards minutes the cloud did produce and asks the user
		to do something about a problem that does not exist.

config VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_BLOCK_BYTES
	int "VelaSight download write block (bytes)"
	default 32768
	range 512 262144
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much of a download is accumulated before it is written to the
		card.  Must be a multiple of 512; the point of the option is
		sector alignment and an odd value defeats it.

		This exists because of how two layers meet.  The TLS transport
		hands the download sink one record at a time -- mbedtls_ssl_read()
		returns at most one record however large a buffer it is given,
		about 1.4 KB on a 1500-byte path -- and this filesystem is FAT on
		SD-NAND with a 1-bit bus, single-block PIO transfers and no write
		buffer beneath it.  Writing each record straight through therefore
		straddles 512-byte sectors at both ends, and FAT reads back and
		rewrites the partial ones; the next record then reads the same
		trailing sector again.

		Measured 2026-09-07 without aggregation: 114054 bytes took 62.6 s,
		about 0.77 s per record, 1.8 KB/s.  Uploads to the same host over
		the same pooled connection in the same session ran near 24 KB/s
		and write nothing, so the cost was the card rather than the link.
		It was slow enough that the transfer died at 62 s.

		That death was described here as the peer resetting the
		connection, which was wrong.  The log line was
		"ssl_read (stream) ret=0x4c", and MBEDTLS_ERR_NET_RECV_FAILED is
		-0x004C while MBEDTLS_ERR_NET_CONN_RESET is -0x0050.  It was a
		local read timeout: vela_tls.c puts the socket in blocking mode
		and bounds it with SO_RCVTIMEO, and mbedtls's net_would_block()
		only maps EAGAIN to "would block" on a non-blocking descriptor,
		so an expired receive timeout surfaces as a read failure.  See
		VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW_BYTES, which is what actually answers
		it.

		32768 is 64 sectors.  Larger mostly buys diminishing returns and
		costs PSRAM for the duration of the transfer; 512 reduces this to
		the unaggregated behaviour without removing the code path.

config VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW_BYTES
	int "VelaSight download window (bytes per GET)"
	default 131072
	range 0 1048576
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much of an object one GET asks for, as a Range request.  Zero
		fetches the whole thing in a single request, which is what this
		code did before the option existed.

		This is here because of a failure three consecutive sessions
		reproduced identically.  Measured 2026-09-07 fetching ttsMinutes
		from the staging store, the transfer starts fast and decays to a
		stop:

		  0      -> 138629 B    2.58 s    53.7 KB/s
		  138629 -> 269701 B   33.20 s     3.9 KB/s
		  269701 -> 294277 B   59.80 s     0.4 KB/s   then it timed out

		and an earlier session did the same at 41.4 KB/s and then
		0.45 KB/s.  The card is not the reason: that last stretch moved
		24 KB, less than one VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_BLOCK_BYTES block, so not
		a single write() happened during those 59.8 seconds.

		What the three runs agree on is that the first ~135 KB of every
		attempt arrives quickly.  So ask for the object in pieces that
		size: each GET finishes inside the fast stretch, and no single
		response lives long enough for the receive timeout to expire.  A
		piece that fails is reissued from the byte count already on the
		card rather than from zero, which is the other half of the fix --
		both failures above threw away everything they had fetched, 179590
		and 294277 bytes of a file around 600 KB.

		131072 sits comfortably inside the fast stretch and is a multiple
		of the write block, so window boundaries do not create unaligned
		writes.  The extra requests share the pooled connection, so they
		cost a request line each and no handshake.

		A store that signs its header set may refuse a Range header the
		way it refuses an unexpected Content-Type -- see
		VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_CONTENT_TYPE.  That is handled rather than
		configured: a 400, 403 or 501 on the first window drops to a
		single unwindowed request and says so in the log.

config VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_ATTEMPTS
	int "VelaSight download attempts per window"
	default 4
	range 1 16
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many consecutive transport failures a windowed download
		tolerates before it gives up.  Only meaningful when
		VS_SOCIAL_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW_BYTES is non-zero, since without Range
		there is no way to ask for the rest of an object.

		Consecutive, and reset only by a completed response.  A failed
		window keeps the bytes it did deliver and the next request resumes
		past them, so every attempt makes progress whether or not it
		succeeds -- which means the count does not need to distinguish
		"slow" from "broken" to terminate.  Refilling the budget on
		partial delivery instead would not terminate at all: a window that
		hands over one byte and then stalls forever would reset it forever.

		Note that the finalizing page has its own bound over the top of
		this one, VS_SOCIAL_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS, and that a download retrying
		into a wall will hit that first.

config VS_TTS_CHUNK_BYTES
	int "VelaSight audio playback read block (bytes)"
	default 16384
	range 4096 262144
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How much of a WAV file is read per iteration while playing it, and
		therefore how much memory playback holds.

		Sized against the card, not the audio.  The constraint is that a
		block must be read in less time than it takes to play, or the DAC
		starves and the listener hears gaps.

		The storage this was written against no longer exists.  It was
		8192, raised to 65536 when a 1.4 KB write measured 0.77 s and the
		card supplied 8.9 KB/s -- 28% of the 32 KB/s that 16 kHz 16-bit
		mono consumes, so nothing could be streamed and the file had to be
		read in full first.  After the SD-NAND fix the same read path
		measures 605 KB/s (486400 bytes in 803 ms, 2026-09-07), which is
		19 times what playback needs.

		So the block no longer has to be large, and being large now costs
		something: it is held for the length of the file. 16384 is 512 ms
		of this audio and 32 sectors exactly, read in about 27 ms against
		the 512 ms it plays for.  That is still a 19x margin -- the ratio
		is set by the card against the sample rate and does not change with
		the block size -- while holding a quarter of what 65536 did.

		The cost is reaction time.  A stop is acted on at block
		boundaries, so the read loop leaves up to one block late -- which
		nobody hears, because vs_audio_playback_stop() silences the DAC
		immediately and independently of this loop.  A smaller block makes
		that shorter, not longer.

config VS_TTS_PRELOAD_MAX_BYTES
	int "VelaSight audio preload budget (bytes)"
	default 0
	range 0 4194304
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Largest spoken-minutes file that is read into memory in full before
		the speaker is opened.  Larger files are streamed instead.

		Zero, which means never preload and always stream.  Any larger
		value reads a file up to that size into memory before opening the
		DAC.

		This existed because the storage could not feed realtime audio.
		Measured 2026-09-07 before the SD-NAND fix: 199680 bytes took
		22467 ms to read, 8.9 KB/s, against the 32 KB/s that 16 kHz 16-bit
		mono consumes -- 28% of what playback needed, so a streamed file
		underran by arithmetic rather than by bad luck and the gaps fell in
		the middle of the sentence.  Reading the whole file first did not
		make playback finish sooner and could not; it moved the waiting to
		the front, where it was a pause before the summary instead of holes
		inside it.

		The premise is gone.  The same read path now measures 605 KB/s
		(486400 bytes in 803 ms), 19 times what playback consumes, so
		streaming through VS_TTS_CHUNK_BYTES has ample margin and was
		measured with 0 underruns.

		Preloading has a cost that only became visible once it worked.  On
		the run that first played a file end to end, the 486400-byte
		preload left "psram heap free 103008 largest 102888 of 3014656" --
		3.4% of the PSRAM heap -- while the playback ring held another
		327680.  The budget above it was 1048576, so a file only slightly
		larger would have failed to allocate, fallen back to streaming
		anyway, and reported it as an out-of-memory condition.  Streaming
		unconditionally removes both the peak and that cliff, along with
		the 803 ms of silence in front of the audio.

		Raise it above zero only if a card regresses to the speeds
		described above.

config VS_SOCIAL_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight spoken minutes download timeout (ms)"
	default 90000
	range 10000 300000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long the finalizing page waits for the spoken minutes to
		download before treating the transfer as wedged.

		Not enforced by the download itself -- the transport already ends
		a stalled connection on VS_SOCIAL_IO_TIMEOUT_MS, and a transfer
		that is merely slow is still progress.  This is the UI's
		no-progress deadline for that step, and it is deliberately the
		longest of them: the file's size is not known in advance and it is
		the one step whose duration scales with how much the other side
		had to say.

		Sized against measurement rather than guessed.  On 2026-09-07 a
		138 KB prefix took 16.4 s, and that was with the camera still
		streaming and taking CPU from the transfer; the camera is now
		released before this step runs.  90 s covers a file several times
		that size on a link several times worse.

config VS_HISTORY_TTS_DWELL_MS
	int "VelaSight history auto-play dwell (ms)"
	default 2000
	range 500 10000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long a record has to stay on screen before its spoken minutes
		start playing.

		The delay is the whole feature.  Browsing is a held key producing
		a record every couple of hundred milliseconds, and starting audio
		on arrival would mean a burst of first syllables; starting it only
		once the user has stopped turns "I am looking at this one" into the
		trigger.  Every change of selection restarts this and silences
		whatever was playing.

config VS_TTS_STACKSIZE
	int "VelaSight audio playback worker stack (bytes)"
	default 8192
	range 4096 32768
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Stack for the thread that reads a WAV file and feeds the DAC.

		Small on purpose: the transfer buffer is drawn from PSRAM rather
		than kept here, so what is left is a RIFF header parse and a read
		loop.

config VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_DEBOUNCE_WINDOWS
	int "VelaSight social alert confirmation readings"
	default 2
	range 1 10
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many extreme readings raise an alert, and how many consecutive
		calm ones clear it.

		Without this the screen changes colour on single-reading noise.
		The cloud itself does not debounce at all -- it opens an advice
		slot on the first red frame -- so this is the device deciding that
		one reading is not enough to interrupt someone.

		"Readings", not "frames", and not "consecutive" on the raise side.
		It used to require N extreme results in a row and was reset by any
		result that was not extreme, which measurement showed asks for far
		more than it appears to: 43 images uploaded produced 7 emotion
		results, about one every 9.6 s, so three in a row meant anger
		detected continuously across roughly nineteen seconds with no
		misclassification anywhere in between -- and one stray green in the
		middle of a real argument reset the count to zero.  The raise side
		now counts readings inside
		VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_WINDOW_MS instead.

		2 against a cloud that would fire on 1: two independent readings
		agreeing is the least this can ask for and still be a filter, and
		with the result yield above, three is close to unreachable.

		The clear side is still a consecutive count, because a calm streak
		is only the fast path there -- VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_HOLD_MS is what
		actually releases an alert.

config VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_WINDOW_MS
	int "VelaSight social alert confirmation window (ms)"
	default 20000
	range 1000 120000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How far apart the VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_DEBOUNCE_WINDOWS extreme readings
		may be and still count as one moment.

		This is the span the raise decision looks back over.  A reading
		that is not extreme does not clear the count; the window expiring
		is what forgets, which is what makes the filter tolerant of the
		occasional misread without making it tolerant of anger that
		happened a minute ago.

		20000 is sized against the measured result rate rather than
		against the sampling rate, because the two are far apart: the
		sampler runs at 500-2000 ms but only about one image in six comes
		back classified, so consecutive readings are ~10 s apart.  Two of
		them inside 20 s is reachable; the same two inside 2 s is not.

		Shorten it if the cloud's classification yield improves, which is
		the number that really governs this feature.

config VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_MIN_CONFIDENCE
	int "VelaSight social alert minimum confidence (percent)"
	default 40
	range 0 100
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How sure the cloud has to be about an extreme reading before it
		counts towards raising an alert.

		The interface sends a confidence with every emotion result -- as a
		string, "0.95" -- and the device parsed it, carried it and then
		never looked at it, so a red at 0.5 weighed the same as one at
		0.95 in a decision made from only a couple of readings.

		Applies to raising only.  A low-confidence red still counts as
		"still extreme" for VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_HOLD_MS, so a run of unsure
		readings holds an existing alert open without being able to start
		one.  Raising interrupts the user; holding does not.

		A result with no confidence field at all is always accepted: a
		server that omits it must not silently switch the feature off.

		0 disables the check.

		40 is measured, not chosen.  The interface example shows "0.95" and
		this was first set to 60 on the strength of it, which turned out to
		be above the entire range this model actually produces.  Two
		sessions, 2026-09-07:

		  R0000021, 28 classified frames: 0.37 to 0.67, median 0.55, none
		  at or above 0.70, only 37% at or above 0.60.
		  19:55 session, every one of the 7 red frames: 40, 42, 44, 45, 46,
		  47, 57.  Highest 57.

		So the floor rejected all seven reds, no alert was raised all
		session, and the one advice the cloud did produce was discarded on
		arrival for want of an alert to attach it to -- the feature could
		not fire at any point.  At 40 those same seven all pass, the second
		of them lands 6.3 s after the first and inside
		VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_WINDOW_MS, and the advice arrives 5.9 s after that
		with the alert still held by VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_COOLDOWN_MS.

		Raise it again if the model's confidence range moves up.  Judge it
		against the "extreme frame ignored, confidence N below M" lines,
		which print the real distribution one line per frame.

config VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_COOLDOWN_MS
	int "VelaSight social alert cooldown (ms)"
	default 8000
	range 2000 60000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Minimum time an alert stays on screen before the calm streak is
		allowed to clear it.

		This is a readability floor, not a filter: the debounce above
		already decides whether the emotion is real.  An alert that
		appeared and vanished before it could be read would be worse than
		none, because the user would know something happened and not what.

config VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_HOLD_MS
	int "VelaSight social alert release timeout (ms)"
	default 12000
	range 2000 120000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long an alert survives without a single extreme frame before it
		releases itself.

		The calm streak above is the fast path and this is the one that
		works.  Clearing on a run of calm results needs the cloud to keep
		answering with faces it judged calm, and that is not what a session
		mostly receives: a frame with no usable face comes back as status
		30 and advances neither streak, and a frame whose result never
		arrives is retired unanswered.  Measured 2026-09-07, 43 uploaded
		images produced 7 emotionTimeline entries and 15 unanswered ids --
		so three consecutive calm results is a coincidence, not an
		expectation, and the user who calmed down and looked away produced
		none of them.

		The symptom was an alert raised once staying on screen for the rest
		of the session.  It never blocked anything -- pause and the hold to
		end both work from the alert page -- but the device went on warning
		about an argument that had finished.

		Evaluated on the poll loop rather than when a result arrives, which
		is the whole point: it has to run when nothing is arriving.  The
		cooldown above still applies, so the effective minimum is whichever
		of the two is longer.

		12000 is six poll intervals and covers a sampler backed off to its
		slowest, where frames are 2 s apart.  Lower it for a device that
		should forget faster; raise it if an alert flickers off during a
		pause in a conversation that is still tense.

		A release also waits for any advice still outstanding, however
		long this is: an alert that cleared first would make its own advice
		arrive stale and be discarded.  VS_SOCIAL_ADVICE_TIMEOUT_MS is what
		bounds that wait.

config VS_SOCIAL_ADVICE_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "VelaSight social advice wait (ms)"
	default 30000
	range 2000 120000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long an extreme frame's message is held waiting for the advice
		the cloud derives from the audio around it.

		The interface delivers that advice under the msgId of the image it
		judged extreme, not under any audio msgId, so the device has to keep
		that image's id in flight to collect it -- and the interface defines
		no terminal state for an advice that will not be sent.  A msgEvent 1
		sub-result goes to 11 and, if the AI produces nothing, stays there.
		Without a bound on this side such an entry is held for the rest of
		the session, and enough of them fill
		VS_SOCIAL_INFLIGHT_MAX with permanent waiters.

		30000 is generous against the observed shape of the exchange: the
		advice needs the audio that follows the extreme moment, which is
		2 s per chunk plus upload plus inference.  It is deliberately longer
		than VS_SOCIAL_ALERT_HOLD_MS, since the alert waits for this rather
		than the other way round.

		Reaching it is reported as "no advice for msg N within M ms".  A
		cloud that answers 30 on the advice instead -- "音频无有效声音" --
		is a proper terminal state and ends the wait immediately.

config VS_SOCIAL_AUDIO_QUEUE_SLOTS
	int "VelaSight social audio upload queue depth (chunks)"
	default 16
	range 4 64
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many encoded audio chunks may wait for the uploader.

		Audio and images used to share one four-slot ring, which meant a
		burst of frames could evict speech.  They cannot substitute for
		each other: a dropped frame costs one sample out of a timeline
		that has many, while a dropped chunk removes those seconds of the
		conversation from the transcript and from the spoken minutes
		permanently -- nothing else recorded them.  So audio now has its
		own queue, and it is deep.

		Deep is affordable here precisely because audio is small.  One
		chunk is CONFIG_VS_SOCIAL_AUDIO_CHUNK_MS of Opus at
		CONFIG_VS_SOCIAL_AUDIO_BITRATE, about 6 KB for the default two
		seconds at 24 kbps, so sixteen slots reserve roughly 96 KB of
		PSRAM worst case and buy 32 seconds of stall tolerance against a
		producer that emits one chunk every two seconds.

		This is a depth, not a promise.  A stall longer than the depth
		still drops the oldest chunk, because the alternative is blocking
		the thread that drains the microphone ring and losing the audio
		one level lower down where nothing counts it.  Every such drop is
		logged individually rather than coalesced.

config VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_QUEUE_SLOTS
	int "VelaSight social image upload queue depth (frames)"
	default 2
	range 1 8
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many captured frames may wait for the uploader.  Full means
		the oldest is dropped and the sampler slows down.

		Deliberately shallow, and shallower than the four slots images
		used to share with audio.  Each slot can hold 160 KB of PSRAM,
		and an emotion result is about the moment it was sampled: a queue
		that holds a backlog spends the uplink on faces that have stopped
		being current.  Two slots means what reaches the cloud is the
		newest frame plus at most one predecessor.

		Overflow is not only absorbed here, it is fed back:
		CONFIG_VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MAX_MS describes how far the
		sampler backs off when this fills.

config VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MAX_MS
	int "VelaSight social image sampling floor when the queue backs up (ms)"
	default 2000
	range 500 5000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		The slowest the image sampler will go, in milliseconds between
		frames.  2000 is 0.5 fps.

		CONFIG_VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS is the other end: the fastest,
		and where a session starts.  Between them the sampler moves
		itself.  A frame dropped for a full queue doubles the interval; a
		run of frames that uploaded without a drop shortens it again in
		smaller steps.  Backing off multiplicatively and recovering
		additively is the same shape as TCP congestion control and for
		the same reason -- overshooting the link's capacity should be
		expensive to do and cheap to undo.

		What this buys is not throughput, it is freshness.  Sampling
		faster than the uplink can carry does not deliver more frames, it
		delivers the same number with a backlog in front of them, so every
		frame the cloud sees is older than it had to be.  Matching the
		sampler to the link keeps the newest frame the one being sent.

		Raising this lets a bad link degrade further before frames are
		lost outright; lowering it toward
		CONFIG_VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS disables the adaptation.

config VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_BACKOFF_MIN_MS
	int "VelaSight social upload backoff floor (ms)"
	default 250
	range 50 5000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How long the upload thread sleeps after the first upload failure
		in a row, before trying the next queued item.  Doubles on each
		further consecutive failure up to
		VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_BACKOFF_MAX_MS, and resets to no wait the moment
		an upload succeeds.

		vs_cloud_social_upload() folds every failure into one negative
		return -- a refused register call, a presigned PUT the object
		store 403s on a signature mismatch, a timed-out connect, a
		malformed response -- and this does not distinguish them: none is
		fixed by asking again with no delay.

		Without this the upload thread is a loop with no wait of its own,
		bounded only by round-trip latency.  It ran at VS_PRIORITY_VOICE
		when this was written, above the UI; it now runs at
		VS_PRIORITY_SOCIAL, below it, so the starvation described next
		can no longer reach the renderer -- but the wasted round trips
		are still wasted.  Measured 2026-09-01: a store that
		403s every attempt (signature mismatch is deterministic per
		request shape, so this is not a rare transient) drove that loop
		at up to five attempts a second, which is CPU time taken from
		every lower-priority task for as long as the queue kept handing
		it work -- including input polling, so key presses stopped
		registering while the session kept running and logging.

		Kept close to VS_SOCIAL_IMAGE_INTERVAL_MS so one isolated failure
		costs about one sample period rather than a visible stall.

config VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_BACKOFF_MAX_MS
	int "VelaSight social upload backoff ceiling (ms)"
	default 8000
	range 1000 300000
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Upper bound the doubling in VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_BACKOFF_MIN_MS's
		help text stops at.  A store stuck failing every request then
		spends most of its time asleep rather than retrying into a wall.

		Raised from 8000 after measuring what the old ceiling actually
		bought.  Observed 2026-08-31 with the ceiling at 8 s: refusals
		arrived about every 3.4 s for the whole session, because the
		doubling starts over on any answer that is not a failure and each
		attempt costs a TLS round trip before it can fail.  8 s was never
		reached often enough to matter; the loop stayed at a few seconds
		indefinitely.

		It was briefly 60000, on the reading that the refusals were a
		destination that had genuinely gone away.  That reading was
		wrong: they were the load balancer answering from the instance
		that did not hold the session, which vs_cloud.c now avoids by
		keeping the connection that opened it.  Waiting a minute for a
		routing decision to change would have been a minute spent on
		nothing, so this is back to a few seconds -- long enough for a
		transient network or store problem, which is what it is for.

config VS_SOCIAL_DEAD_SESSION_STRIKES
	int "VelaSight social consecutive session-gone refusals before stopping"
	default 12
	range 1 64
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many upload attempts in a row may come back as status 30 --
		"session not found or closed" -- before the upload thread stops
		and asks the session to shut down.

		This was 3, chosen while status 30 was believed to mean the
		session had genuinely gone.  It does not.  Measured 2026-09-03
		with curl from a host, so nothing to do with this device: a fresh
		connection per request failed 16 times in 30 because the balancer
		routes per connection and only one of the two instances holds the
		session, while an open plus ten uploads plus the close on a single
		connection succeeded 33 times out of 33.

		At that failure rate a run of three arrived about every seven
		uploads, so a threshold of 3 ended essentially every session
		within the first few seconds -- which is what the board did.  No
		threshold fixes that: the events are independent, so any value
		low enough to detect a real closure is also low enough to trip on
		the routing.  vs_cloud.c holds the connection that opened the
		session instead, which removes the refusals rather than counting
		them.

		12 is therefore a backstop rather than a detector.  With the
		connection held, a status 30 means something genuinely changed on
		the far side -- the instance restarted, or another open superseded
		this session -- and twelve in a row is unambiguous.  It is not
		load-bearing for the routing problem any more.

		Any successful upload resets the count: what ends a session is a
		run of refusals, not a total across it.

config VS_SOCIAL_INFLIGHT_MAX
	int "VelaSight social in-flight message tracking"
	default 16
	range 4 32
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many uploaded msgIds are tracked while waiting for results.

		Sized from the uploads that can be outstanding within one poll
		interval, with room to spare.  When it is full the oldest entry is
		retired unanswered, which loses one frame's emotion result and
		nothing else -- the cloud still has the data and still counts it
		in the end-of-session timeline.

# Stacks, one option per session thread.
#
# These were a single shared option, which cannot work: the four threads differ
# by a large factor in what they need, so one value is either too small for the
# worst of them or many times too large for the rest.  It was 8192 while the
# session thread wanted 12 KiB in a single frame, and the hardware stack limit
# turned that into a UsageFault three milliseconds into every session --
# measured 2026-09-01, from the assertion dump rather than inferred.
#
# They come from PSRAM, not from the SRAM heap as the help text here used to
# claim.  The same dump put the session thread's stack at 0x60a1eb10, inside
# PSRAM_SECTION, along with the other three.  So being generous costs room in a
# 6 MB region that holds only framebuffers and these stacks, and costs nothing
# in the SRAM the kernel is actually short of.  All four are claimed only while
# a session is running.

config VS_SOCIAL_STACKSIZE_SESSION
	int "VelaSight social session-thread stack size"
	default 16384
	range 4096 65536
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		The orchestrator: opens the devices, spawns the other three, polls
		for results and builds the closing minutes.

		Sized for the closing sequence rather than for the polling loop.
		cJSON parsing of a full end-of-session response, with both
		timelines, recurses, and social_finalize_sequence() alone measures
		about 1 KiB of frame.

		social_poll_once() used to dominate this thread at 12 KiB in one
		frame -- 32 vs_social_event_s of 364 bytes each.  That array is now
		allocated from PSRAM once per session, so this no longer has to
		cover it.

config VS_SOCIAL_STACKSIZE_AUDIO
	int "VelaSight social audio-thread stack size"
	default 32768
	range 4096 65536
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		The largest of the four, and not by a small margin, because it is
		the one that runs libopus.

		libopus is built here with VAR_ARRAYS, so its working buffers land
		on the caller's stack rather than inside the encoder state object.
		app/audio_test, the only other user of this same encoder, records
		from measurement that 8 KiB was not enough and uses 32768.

		Note that the encoder *state* already comes from PSRAM.  That does
		nothing for the stack, which is the part VAR_ARRAYS moves.

config VS_SOCIAL_STACKSIZE_UPLOAD
	int "VelaSight social upload-thread stack size"
	default 16384
	range 4096 65536
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		Pops the ring and does the cloud round trip: HTTP request assembly,
		optionally TLS, then a JSON response to parse.  Its own frame is
		trivial and everything it needs sits below it.

		Costed per worker; see VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_WORKERS.

config VS_SOCIAL_UPLOAD_WORKERS
	int "VelaSight social upload workers"
	default 2
	range 1 2
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		How many threads pop the upload rings.  Each costs
		VS_SOCIAL_STACKSIZE_UPLOAD.

		More than one because an upload is two serial round trips to two
		different hosts -- register with the business server, then PUT the
		bytes to the presigned URL -- and one worker can only be inside one
		of them at a time.  Measured 2026-09-07: 984 ms per item, against a
		budget of two pooled round trips at about 135 ms each.

		The cost is also very nearly independent of payload size, which is
		what says the limit is latency rather than bandwidth: in one
		session an image-heavy report window averaged 869 ms per item while
		an audio-heavy one, carrying a quarter of the bytes, averaged
		1636 ms.  Bandwidth would have made the small items faster.
		Latency does not care, and latency is what overlapping requests
		hides.

		The two registrations still take turns, because they share one
		cleartext connection that vs_cloud.c serializes -- deliberately, so
		that the connection which opened the session stays the one every
		request uses.  What overlaps is one item's registration against
		another item's transfer.

		Two is the ceiling, and it is set outside this application.
		vela_tls.c pools connections in CONN_POOL_SIZE slots, two by
		default, and reads every response through one of TLS_RAW_BUF_COUNT
		static 8 KB buffers, also two.  A third concurrent transfer would
		find no pool slot and open an unpooled connection -- a full
		handshake per upload -- and would block waiting for a response
		buffer.  Raising it here without raising those would be slower, not
		faster.

		Set to 1 to get the old single-worker behaviour.  A session still
		runs if only some workers start: the rest are throughput, and only
		failing to start any is fatal.

config VS_SOCIAL_STACKSIZE_CAPTURE
	int "VelaSight social capture-thread stack size"
	default 8192
	range 4096 65536
	depends on LVX_USE_DEMO_CONTEST2026_264_VELASIGHT
	---help---
		The shallowest of the four: a poll, an ioctl and a copy, with a
		measured frame of 24 bytes.  Left at the old shared default because
		there is no reason to trim it, and against a frame that shallow the
		whole value is headroom.
